            <ArticleTitle>[Topical methylprednisolone vs lidocaïne for the prevention of postoperative sore throat].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>595-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">We assessed the efficacy of topical methylprednisolone or lidocaine for prevention of postoperative sore throat.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Randomised, prospective in single blind study.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENTS AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Sixty patients ASA 1 or 2 undergoing tracheal intubation for dental surgery received before intubation either topical lidocaine 5% (15 puffs) or aerosolized methylprednisolone (80 mg). Postoperative pain was assessed by the patients using a VAS and a specific scoring system for sore throat, cough and hoarseness. Evaluations were performed immediately after emergence from anaesthesia, 1 h later, at time of the first postoperative drink, at time of the first postoperative meal and 24 h after surgery.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Patients receiving methylprednisolone showed slightly better scores for sore throat and cough 1 h after surgery.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Topical methylprednisolone may therefore be a useful adjuvant in the prevention of sore throat after intubation.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
